[orch:err] ### milvus-io/milvus#51641 — fix: guard copy-segment state transitions against stale snapshots
[orch:err] - State: `OPEN` · Closing-link from PR: no · Source: `cross`
[orch:err] - URL: https://github.com/milvus-io/milvus/pull/51641
[orch:err] - Author: xiaocai2333
[orch:err] - Labels: kind/bug, size/XXL, ci-passed, dco-passed
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] #### Issue body
[orch:err] issue: #51191
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Follow-up to #51527, addressing the two `nit (non-blocking)` review comments left open when that PR merged. Both are the same defect class the P1 fix in #51527 already solved for the `Pending -> Executing` transition (`UpdateJobInState`): a state transition applied from a **stale snapshot**, overwriting an outcome a concurrent path already committed.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### 1. `tryTimeoutJob` could overwrite a terminal transition
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The checker loop pushes a single job snapshot through `checkCopyingJob(job)` — which may `finishJob` and persist `Completed` — and then `tryTimeoutJob(job)` **in the same round** (`copy_segment_checker.go:163-172`). The latter still holds the pre-loop `Executing` snapshot, so it passes its own state switch, and once the deadline has elapsed it called `UpdateJobStateAndReleaseRef(...Failed)`, which applied the action unconditionally. A restore that had just completed successfully was reported to the user as `Failed`.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The existing `wasTerminal` logic only guarded the *unpin* side effect, not the state mutation, so it did not prevent this.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] `UpdateJobStateAndReleaseRef` now skips the update when the **current cached** job is already terminal. Every caller of this method drives a non-terminal job to `Completed`/`Failed`, so observing a terminal job here always means a concurrent path won the race and this caller is stale — there is no legitimate terminal -> terminal transition. The check and the mutate share `m.mu`, so the decision cannot go stale between them.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Guarding at the meta layer rather than in `tryTimeoutJob` is deliberate: the check must be atomic with the mutate, and the lock lives here — a re-read inside `tryTimeoutJob` would still be TOCTOU. It also hardens `markTaskAndJobFailed` and `finishJob` against the same race.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### 2. Worker-loss reset did not check the parent job state
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] On confirmed worker loss, `QueryTaskOnWorker` reset the task to `Pending`/`NullNodeID` via an unconditional `UpdateTask`. That response can land long after the query was issued, by which time another task may have failed the parent job. Reviving the task then let the global scheduler issue one more dispatch for an already-dead job before `checkFailedJob` converged it back to `Failed`.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The reset now goes through the new `CopySegmentMeta.UpdateTaskInStateIfJobActive`, which applies the actions only if the task is still `InProgress` **and** its parent job is still active, with both checks and the update under the same write lock.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### Testing
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] 9 new tests, all passing:
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] - `TestTryTimeoutJob_DoesNotOverwriteTerminalJob` — the exact stale-snapshot scenario from the review
[orch:err] - `TestQueryTaskOnWorker_WorkerLossDoesNotResetWhenJobTerminal` — delayed worker-loss response against a Failed job
[orch:err] - `TestUpdateJobStateAndReleaseRef_SkipsTerminalJob` / `_AppliesOnNonTerminalJob` — guard blocks the resurrection but not the legitimate `Executing -> Failed` timeout transition
[orch:err] - `TestUpdateTaskInStateIfJobActive_{AppliesWhenJobActive,SkipsWhenJobTerminal,SkipsWhenTaskStateMismatch,MissingTaskOrJob,CatalogErrorLeavesTaskUnchanged}`
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Each of the four regression tests was mutation-checked: with the corresponding guard neutered they fail, and pass once it is restored — so they exercise the defect rather than passing vacuously.
[orch:err] 
